            <ArticleTitle>Chronic illness care in Russia: a pilot project to improve asthma care in a &quot;closed city&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>861-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In Russia, where health status has deteriorated since the late 1960s, asthma is a growing medical and public health problem.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To create a model for improving care and outcomes of patients with asthma through altering the culture of health care, physician behavior, and public policy in a Russian community.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 6-month, nonrandomized, before-and-after intervention evaluation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatients of Medical-Sanitary Unit No. 50, the central health authority in Sarov, Russia, a &quot;closed&quot; nuclear city.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A consecutive sample of 85 adult patients with severe-persistent or moderate-persistent asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A comprehensive asthma-care program that emphasized patient education and self-management with treatment based on internationally accepted guidelines modified for local resources.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Missed work or school, patients requiring emergency department visits, number of patients hospitalized, daytime symptoms, nighttime symptoms, rescue inhaler use, patient satisfaction, and FEV(1).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After 6 months, significant reductions were observed in the proportion of patients missing work or school (1.2% vs 11.8%), emergency department visits (4.8% vs 15.7%), hospitalizations (0% vs 9.4%), daily symptoms (47.1% vs 65.9%), and nightly symptoms (14.1% vs 37.6%). Patient satisfaction with asthma control (81.2% vs 31.8%) and average level of FEV(1) (84.0% vs 72.4%) significantly increased from baseline.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The model for changing asthma management in this Russian community was effective in improving asthma outcomes and offers a reproducible paradigm approach for improving chronic illness care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
